<%#
  The project settings tab (curator decision 2026-08-21).

  Redmine's own chrome only: `fieldset.box`, `legend`, `p`, `p.nodata`, `.warning`.
  CLAUDE.md §4 forbids plugin-local design tokens in chrome, and there are none here.

  --- NO PLUGIN HELPER IS CALLED FROM THIS FILE, DELIBERATELY ---

  Redmine sets `include_all_helpers = false`, so a partial rendered by a CORE controller sees
  core's helper set and not ours. `reporter_dashboard_icon` — this plugin's 5.1-vs-6 icon
  shim, used by every one of its own views — is therefore unavailable here, and calling it
  would be a NameError on the project settings page rather than on any page we test. Hence
  plain text links: identical on 5.1 through 7.0, and nothing to shim.

  For the same reason the data comes from a fully-qualified lib module rather than a helper.

  --- AND WHY IT IS COUNTS RATHER THAN THE LISTS THEMSELVES ---

  `common/_tabs.html.erb` renders every tab's partial on every request and hides the
  unselected ones with CSS. Somebody renaming the project pays for whatever is in here, so
  what is in here is four statements at most and constant in the size of the data. The work
  surfaces keep their own paginated pages; this links to them. See SettingsTab's header.
%>
<%
  project = @project
  actor = User.current
  summary = RedmineReporterDashboards::SettingsTab.summary(project, actor)
%>

<% if RedmineReporterDashboards::SettingsTab.allowed?(:dashboard, project, actor) %>
  <fieldset class="box">
    <legend><%= l(:label_reporter_dashboard_tabs) %></legend>
    <p><%= l(:text_reporter_settings_dashboard_tabs, count: summary.dashboard_tabs) %></p>
    <%# THE TABS THEMSELVES ARE EDITED ON THE DASHBOARD, not here. Two places to rename a
        tab is two places for the permission check to drift, and the dashboard already has
        the gear that does it.

        SEPARATELY GATED, and not on the section's own permission. This section is opened by
        `manage_reporter_project_tabs`; OPENING the dashboard needs
        `view_reporter_project_page`, and Redmine lets an administrator grant either without
        the other. Reusing the section's check would have offered a link to a 403 — rare, and
        exactly the kind of rare a role audit produces. %>
    <% if actor.allowed_to?(:view_reporter_project_page, project) %>
      <p><%= link_to l(:label_reporter_project_page),
                     project_reporter_page_path(project) %></p>
    <% end %>
  </fieldset>
<% end %>

<% if RedmineReporterDashboards::SettingsTab.allowed?(:templates, project, actor) %>
  <fieldset class="box">
    <legend><%= l(:label_reporter_template_plural) %></legend>
    <p><%= l(:text_reporter_settings_templates, count: summary.templates) %></p>
    <p><%= link_to l(:label_reporter_template_plural),
                   project_reporter_templates_path(project) %></p>
  </fieldset>
<% end %>

<% if RedmineReporterDashboards::SettingsTab.allowed?(:schedules, project, actor) %>
  <fieldset class="box">
    <legend><%= l(:label_reporter_schedule_plural) %></legend>
    <%# FR-44 IN THE SECOND PLACE SOMEBODY LOOKS, through the SAME `Status` and the same two
        keys the schedules index uses — so "the scheduler is not running" cannot come to read
        differently in the two places an operator might see it. %>
    <% if summary.schedules.warning? %>
      <div class="warning">
        <p><strong><%= l(:label_reporter_schedule_not_running) %></strong></p>
        <p><%= l(:text_reporter_schedule_needs_cron) %></p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
    <p><%= l(:text_reporter_settings_schedules, count: summary.schedules.enabled_count) %></p>
    <% if summary.schedules.last_attempt_at %>
      <p>
        <%= l(:label_reporter_schedule_last_run) %>:
        <%= format_time(summary.schedules.last_attempt_at) %>
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <p><%= link_to l(:label_reporter_schedule_plural),
                   project_reporter_schedules_path(project) %></p>
  </fieldset>
<% end %>

<% if RedmineReporterDashboards::SettingsTab.allowed?(:mail, project, actor) %>
  <fieldset class="box">
    <legend><%= l(:label_reporter_adhoc_mail_audit) %></legend>
    <%# NO COUNT HERE, and that is not an omission. `mail#index` shows an admin the whole
        project's sends and everybody else only their own; reproducing that rule for a
        number would put the disclosure decision in two files. The link goes to the page
        that owns it. %>
    <p><%= link_to l(:label_reporter_adhoc_mail_audit),
                   project_reporter_mail_sends_path(project) %></p>
  </fieldset>
<% end %>
